    "summary": "AT&T's war against the way cable operators are marketing hybrid fiber/coax (HFC) is firmly in the name-calling stage. AT&T argues that Charter is using the language to affix a \"fiber halo\" to its marketing of consumer broadband, and that the ongoing use of it is causing \"irreparable harm\" to AT&T, which plans to reach about 60 million locations with FTTP by 2030 (via its own buildouts and through partnerships). In its move last month to have the case dismissed, Spectrum held that there's nothing false or misleading about Charter's use of \"fiber-powered,\" noting that it has been clear about describing the differences between HFC and FTTP, using \"fiber-powered\" for HFC and \"100% fiber\" where it has deployed FTTP. Related: AT&T's 'fiber-powered' crusade expands to Comcast Meanwhile, DSL used to be one of cable's favorite targets, with jabs that included Comcast's \"Slowskys\" campaign years ago when it was clear that DSL had no shot at keeping pace with DOCSIS.",
