    "publishedAt": "2026-11-26",
    "summary": "Pentagon leaders considered using the Cold War-era Defense Production Act to pressure Nvidia to reinstate founder Jensen Huang as CEO following his resignation over an affair with a suspected Chinese spy, with The Defense Production Act is a 1950 law that allows a president to intervene in the economy and is usually used to prompt companies to supply the military during wartime. The law is usually used to force those companies to supply the military during wartime, but Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and his senior deputy, Steve Feinberg, wondered whether it could also be used to put pressure on Nvidia's board. While the DFA doesn't explicitly give the president the power to place a particular person into the CEO role at a company, it could still be wielded to achieve the same result. Such a move would have limited Nvidia's ability to sell components for a profit, affecting its production plans and share price as a result.",
