    "importance": "中",
    "sourceId": "tomshardware",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/artificial-intelligence/export-banned-nvidia-blackwell-b300-servers-are-openly-for-sale-in-china-for-up-to-usd3-4-million-a-5x-to-6x-markup-over-u-s-prices",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-26",
    "summary": "Seemingly at the cost of only a bit of conversation, an initially reluctant seller named Wu made a few calls and eventually quoted around $3 million for an eight-GPU server with Blackwell B300 chips, the highest-end version. Equivalent hardware purportedly sells for around $500,000-600,000 in the U.S., but it apparently sells easily even with the apparent 5-6x markup. While inference tasks (running the models) can be performed as well or, arguably better, with efficiency-oriented alternatives, training a frontier-level model requires astounding amounts of compute power, to the tune of thousands of B300s. The fact that those people opt to just fly to China and pay a sizable markup instead of trying to procure the GPUs more directly themselves may illustrate how well-oiled China's grey-market supply chain is.",
    "whyItMatters": "这类信息关系到 AI 服务器从单卡采购转向整机柜交付，立讯应关注电源、散热、线束、连接器和组装复杂度变化。",
