    "id": "real_tomshardware_2026_11_26_tsmc_s_22_6_billion_texas_plan_wouldn_t_make_2nm_or_better_chips_un",
    "title": "TSMC’s $22.6 billion Texas plan wouldn’t make 2nm-or-better chips until 2031",
    "signalCategory": "产品",
    "industry": "核心零部件",
    "topic": "",
    "companies": [
      "TSMC"
    ],
    "importance": "中",
    "sourceId": "tomshardware",
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/semiconductors/tsmc-files-for-usd22-6-billion-texas-fab-campus-with-local-tax-breaks-just-one-county-over-from-musks-terafab-proposed-brazos-county-plant-wont-make-2nm-class-chips-until-2031",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-26",
    "summary": "The proposed fab would be just one county over from Terafab, would likely share supply chains, and would be positioned to make 2nm-class chips by 2031, with the $22.6 billion plan detailing how the Taiwanese chipmaker is planning to expand its U.S. operations. By contrast, the second fab in Arizona is expected to begin volume production in Q3 2027, but at 3nm. For scale, we’ve estimated that a single 2nm-class module with about 20,000 wafer starts a month costs $25 billion to $35 billion, so this plan’s $22.6 billion budget falls below the low end of that range. The price tag is split in multiple ways, with $8.60 billion for buildings and other real property, while 62% of the total, or $14.02 billion, is for tooling, machinery and equipment, a category that on the filing’s list also covers power, HVAC, cleanroom, water and gas systems.",
    "whyItMatters": "先进封装和制程节奏会决定 AI 芯片交付能力，间接影响服务器整机和机柜组件订单能见度。",
